  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis is a scoped structural result: **no new \u03ba, no construction.** It adds only `research/complex-rank8-rigidity-p104-admissible/`. The selected record, `upstream/` and all frozen pins are unchanged.\n\nPR #1212 (burntout76) proves rank-8 gauge rigidity, K = D(2G)D\u207b\u00b9, of the masked (E-graph) system on P112, P120 and the 336 type-(2,2,2,2) P104 units. Its PROOF.md \u00a710 leaves the **896 admissible P104 units** open (PR717's P104 among them). On #1212's pivot there, four classes keep only two live rows. For those classes Lemma 5 leaves a one-parameter family. This PR closes that orbit.\n\n- **What already holds.** On #1212's admissible pivot, all 14 blocks carry a complete class, so Lemmas 5\u20136 fix the global scaling `g` on the 22 complete classes unchanged. #1212 \u00a710 records that (I2), (I3) and (I4) hold; only (I1) fails.\n- **New: Lemma 9 (propagation).** A two-row line `i` needs no in-class argument. Take a witness `j` in a complete class with `T_i \u2229 T_j = {k, k\u2032}`. Then (E2) gives `a_ik \u03b5_ik / g_k = a_ik\u2032 \u03b5_ik\u2032 / g_k\u2032`, and the reverse order gives the same for `b`. If the witnessed pairs connect `T_i`, then `a_i \u221d \u03b5_i \u2218 g` and `b_i \u221d \u03b5_i / g`, and (E0) fixes the product. On this unit, every two-row line has all six pairs witnessed, with 40 witnesses each. The lemma is linear, needs no zero exclusion for `i`, and uses no equation inside its class.\n\n**Consequence.** In the E-graph model, every decoder with exactly eight costly terms has cst \u2265 56 on every admissible P104 unit, and PR717's P104 decoders attain it. With #1212 (type (2,2,2,2)) and #1389 (type (4,4), unmerged), rigidity then covers all 1260 P104 frame complements.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `python3 -I -B research/complex-rank8-rigidity-p104-admissible/verify.py` runs 11 exact checks and 4 controls in about 0.1 s. It is an independent implementation (own E8 model, classes, witnesses, gluing, propagation) and imports nothing from #1212. It recomputes the orbit type of the punctured frame. It cross-checks Lemma 9's algebra on the actual (E2) equations: rank 3, with the gauge vector as kernel. The controls show:\n  - in-class conditions admit a non-gauge two-row solution;\n  - witnessed cross-block equations reject it (28/40);\n  - removing the witnesses at one column leaves a 2-dimensional family;\n  - a perturbed entry breaks (2).\n- `--pr1212-dir <checkout of 57099cb5>` checks the git blobs of #1212's `verify.py` and `proof-P104-11111111.drup`. It replays `G.P104-11111111.zero-exclusion`, `G.P104-11111111.class-witnesses`, `G.frame-orbits` and `G.pr717-p104-orbit`, and matches the unit and pivot by root vectors. That is 22 checks, all passing locally.\n- Passes on Python 3.10.21, 3.12.14 and 3.14.7. `scripts/research_package.py`: the metadata check and the Python 3.12 registered replay pass. `test_research_*` passes.\n\n## Not checked or not covered\n\n- **Nine or more costly terms** (PR717's mixed-\u03b2 problem).\n- **The intermediate punctures P105\u2013P110.** This is the package's open next question. Lemma 9 makes incomplete classes free once `g` is fixed, but each orbit needs its own zero-exclusion certificate.\n- **Zero exclusion** is #1212's DRUP result, replayed but not re-derived. PR968's F6 premise is inherited.\n- There has been no independent human review of the written proof.\n- All inherited conditions of the selected record remain.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- **Task / scope:** `complex-masked-decoder` (scope key `2a6f2c726b78f9ae7d26`), refined to the #1212 \u00a710 obligation on the admissible orbit. Chooser nonce `6dba39f7133593e3228f8833f5d6594a`, track complex-mechanisms, priority revision 2.\n- **Heads:** base `bce54fb3f895cbe5cd4c0eb477c3afeed100190f`; depends on #1212 head `57099cb5684f38877f31d149d598801671a633db`. Does not depend on #1389.\n- **Claim:** https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1395. This is the open next question of #1389.\n- **Overlap:** no other claim or PR on global rigidity for the admissible units. The overlap search was refreshed before submission. #1292 and #1367 address local (tangent) rigidity only.\n\n## Credit\n\n- **burntout76 / Brett Halligan (#1212):** the theorem, the proof skeleton, the pivot, zero exclusion and the transport.\n- **themilkman216 (#968):** the eight-term floor.\n- **DreamingOfClouds (#717):** the E-graph model and the P104 decoders.\n- **Navinor (#1292):** local P104 rigidity.\n\nSubstantial AI assistance: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code (proof, verifier, write-up). Submitted by the account holder. Apache-2.0.\n\n**Feedback.** This session was quick because #1212's \u00a710 had recorded exactly which finite input failed on each unit, and #1389's handoff named the next step. A short \"which inputs hold\" table in each package is cheap, and it was the most useful thing in the predecessors.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
